  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a northwest flow aloft prevailing across the \nWestern High Plains downstream of an upper level high centered \nacross the Desert Southwest. Near the surface, a lee side trough of \nlow pressure extends northeast across southeast Colorado into \nnorthwest Kansas.\n\nAnother round of minimal isolated thunderstorm chances (20%) exists\nacross west central Kansas into extreme southwest Kansas this evening\nwith the SREF indicating little change in the overall pattern from\nyesterday with H5 vort maxima associated with a weak shortwave ejecting\neastward out of the northern Colorado Rockies into the Western High\nPlains. Near the surface, prevailing southerlies will continue to\nsupport some moisture return across the area, providing minimal\ninstability with MUCAPE values upward of 500 J/kg or so. With \nsteepening mid-level lapse rates, isolated thunderstorm development\ncannot be ruled out toward early evening as ejecting H5 vort maxima\ninteract with an axis of low level convergence associated with the\naforementioned trough extending from extreme southeast Colorado into\nnorthwest Kansas. A few CAMs suggest this while the HREF hints at\nit with 10% probability of 6-hr QPF exceeding only 0.1 of an inch\nacross west central Kansas into extreme southwest Kansas by late\nevening. A secondary focus will be more into central Kansas later\nthis evening, including the I-70 corridor, as thunderstorms likely\ndeveloping in a field of much higher instability in northwest \nKansas/southwest Nebraska track eastward overnight. Similar conditions\nare forecast Monday into Tuesday with little change to the larger\nscale pattern.\n\nVery warm temperatures are forecast again tonight as prevailing \nsoutherlies reinforce a warmer air mass across southwest/central \nKansas. Considering the HREF paints a widespread 70-90% probability \nof temperatures dropping below 80F, look for lows generally in the \n70s(F). With a persistent pattern in place, expect afternoon highs \nback up around 100 to 105F Monday and Tuesday.",
